Coral Jack

Ingredients: NEW JACK SWING / SWINGBEAT + VIBRANT, TROPICAL & SUNNY + Synth Bass
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a salt-crusted 1991 municipal time-capsule cassette unsealed in San Juan, where tape-rot flutter transformed Harlem swingbeat into a sun-baked island groove.

Coral Jack fuses the crisp, swaggering 16th-note bounce of late-1980s swingbeat with radiant Caribbean daylight. It pairs aggressive gated snares and punchy FM synth bass with glistening synthesized steel pans, lush brass stabs, and breezy three-part falsetto harmonies that feel like an inner-city street corner block party hosted on an open-air resort veranda.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 102–110 BPM. Built upon an aggressive 64% swing quantize that pushes the kick and snare into a syncopated strut, layered with fast, rolling live hand percussion (güiro, cabasa, and high-tuned timbales).
- Harmonic Palette: Sun-drenched major 9th, dominant 11th, and suspended soul chords rooted in Mixolydian and Lydian modes, punctuated by bright chromatic turnaround stabs.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Sun-Bleached Tape Saturation: Drum machines and master stems are driven through slightly degraded magnetic tape circuits, introducing subtle warble and harmonic sheen to high frequencies.
  2. The FM "Lately" Slap: Deep, rubbery 4-operator FM synthesizer bass patches layered with a sub-saw wave, executing snappy, syncopated octave leaps.
  3. Gated Lagoon Reverb: Explosive, snappy snare drums treated with tight digital gated plates that simulate the cavernous acoustics of an empty luxury hotel pool pavilion.
- Dynamics: Tracks open with isolated, syncopated synth-bass riffs and shaker ticks, detonate into hyper-vibrant choruses of brass and multi-tracked vocal hooks, drop into a rhythm-only percussive breakdown (known as the "sunbreak"), and ride out on extended, ad-lib-heavy vocal vamps.

Scene & Origin Lore

Coral Jack surfaced along the coastal Condado strip of San Juan in the summer of 2024, ignited when sound engineers unsealed a rusted 1991 municipal time capsule buried in the courtyard of a decommissioned luxury pavilion. Inside rested a water-damaged cassette containing unreleased Harlem swingbeat demos; played back through modern converters, thirty years of sea salt and thermal expansion had warbled the transients and bleached the high end into an intoxicatingly buoyant, humid rhythm. Neighborhood crate-diggers, beach-club keyboardists, and vocalists immediately re-recorded the style, building a micro-scene defined by "Tape-Bake" block parties held on sun-baked docks. The fashion is unmistakable: 1989-style geometric high-top fades bleached blonde by saltwater, pastel silk camp-collar shirts worn open over oversized athletic track pants, chunky gold link chains, and vintage performance sunglasses. Dancers gather in circles on hot sand, executing rapid-fire running-man variations punctuated by salsa footwork.

Typical Instruments Used
Yamaha TX81Z (famous for the punchy "LatelyBass" FM patch), Roland Juno-106, E-mu SP-1200 drum computer, Akai MPC60, digital synthesizer steel pans, Oberheim Matrix-1000 brass patches, live congas, güiro, talkbox, and tight four-part vocal arrangements.

